KG42.register("nodes-20931b9232b325cba7bb5b0a",{"id":"COMP-58a3c8caf66a5d40","code":"PRC032","label":"Endogenous bilateral dual pricing","definition":"In the optimality or equilibrium conditions of the local P2P trading market, endogenously determine the seller-buyer transaction unit price according to the price-dual variable value relationship explicitly stated in the source text.","layer":"L6","template_code":"BCT","concept_id":"pricing.local","concept_ids":["pricing.local"],"identity_dimensions":{"definition":"In the optimality or equilibrium conditions of the local P2P trading market, endogenously determine the seller-buyer transaction unit price according to the price-dual variable value relationship explicitly stated in the source text.","object":"P2P electricity transaction prices identified by buyer, seller, and time period; retain the price-sharing relationship of each source.","inputs":"The trading-market model, optimality conditions, and dual variables specified by the source, as well as bids, costs, duration, and applicable constraints.","operation":"Extract the price from the trading-market solution according to the dual relationship made explicit by the source, retaining the sign, time normalization, and additional price decomposition of that relationship.","outputs":"Market-endogenous bilateral transaction prices and the price bounds specified by the source.","boundary":"Limited to L6/BCT pricing; does not merge complete clearing, allocation, or payment processes. Different objectives, dual-constraint levels, and price-sharing scopes are not treated as equivalent; 49 does not provide the full text of equation (32), and the parent class does not require binding γ to a particular verified balancing constraint. Excludes ex post discounted pricing using a shadow price from a no-trading baseline."},"exclusions":[["Transaction-pair index for buyer j, seller i, and time period t; does not add 49's buyer-level price-sharing relationship.","The bilateral balance is P^{pb}_{j,i,t}+P^{ps}_{i,j,t}=0, and the sold quantity is non-positive; retain the subscript discrepancy of the original text's cross-form sold quantity.","Retain the buying cost, selling revenue, and multiplier-weighted balance term of LMO equation (39), and do not equate them with 49's participant equilibrium objective.","Retain the KKT and the relaxation of constraints (36)–(38) as stated by the source; Λ^{pb} and Λ^{ps} are price parameters and are not conflated with the output λ^c.","Retain the position of τ in each citation, and do not use it to unify the time normalization of the two sources; no explicit sign restriction is given for the price.","t=39 in Fig.12 is an example of price application, not a general pricing time restriction."],["The KKT conditions of all participants' optimization problems are combined to form an equilibrium, and the complementarity conditions are linearized via Big-M to convert to a MILP; this is not equivalent to 43's LMO objective.","Fully retain the seller decomposition λ_sell+λ_cost+(υ−ω)/Δt and the buyer expression −γ_b^t/Δt; the signs and duration factors are not rewritten.","ρ carries s, b, and t indices, but the right-hand side of equation (47) contains only b and t; one must not assume, on the basis of the bilateral indices, that every transaction pair has an independent price degree of freedom.","The price lower bound is FiT plus the transaction fee, and the upper bound is TLOU; the transaction fee includes the network facility usage fee and the aggregator service fee.","A single buyer or seller cannot unilaterally control the equilibrium price; the buyer and seller exit bounds are retained separately.","Allocation to the high-price buyer under scarcity and selection of the low-price seller under surplus are examples of price use; the same-price explanation at t=15 depends on identical parameter settings.","The equilibrium simultaneously produces transaction quantities and SES capacity allocation, but this member projects only price formation and does not include energy-storage capacity prices in the object."]],"members":[{"id":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R006::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","market_meaning":"The submitted total P2P buying-quantity bid limits each individual P2P purchase through the upper bound P^{pb}_{j,t} C_{j,i,t}. Scope: Individual P2P purchases by prosumer j from a different prosumer i at each time t. Condition: Subject to the trading-status factor C_{j,i,t} in (36). The submitted total P2P selling-quantity bid limits each individual P2P sale through the signed lower bound P^{ps}_{j,t}(1 − C_{j,i,t}). Scope: Individual P2P sales by prosumer j to a different prosumer i at each time t. Condition: Subject to the complementary trading-status factor 1 − C_{j,i,t} in (37); selling quantities are nonpositive. The local market operator executes and coordinates P2P trading among prosumers. Scope: The proposed LEM's P2P trading process, including Step 9. The optimal P2P trading price applies to the executed exchange between a buyer and a seller. Scope: P2P exchanges reported in Fig. 12 at the sample time t=39. Combined peer and upstream-grid purchases must not exceed the assigned import DOE. Each peer and grid buying quantity is nonnegative and individually bounded by that DOE. Scope: Prosumer purchases from peers and the upstream grid in each time-slot. Condition: The assigned DOE P̂_{j,t} is positive. Combined signed peer and upstream-grid sales must be at or above the assigned export DOE. Each peer and grid selling quantity lies between that DOE and zero. Scope: Prosumer sales to peers and the upstream grid in each time-slot, using nonpositive signed selling quantities. Condition: The assigned DOE P̂_{j,t} is nonpositive. Total P2P buying and total P2P selling must balance in each time-slot. Scope: Aggregate P2P trading in each time-slot τ. The amount bought by j from i must equal the amount sold by i to j in the same trade and time-slot. Scope: Each individual P2P trade between distinct prosumers j and i at time t. Remaining imports or exports may be traded with the upstream grid. Scope: Residual upstream-grid exchanges included in the LEM. Condition: Imports or exports remain after P2P trading and are within the assigned DOE P̂_{j,t}. The strategy assumes that the grid trade equals the assigned DOE. Scope: Upstream-grid trading included in the proposed LEM strategy. Condition: No P2P trade occurs.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"activation","context_text":"Aggregate P2P trading in each time-slot τ. | Each individual P2P trade between distinct prosumers j and i at time t. | Individual P2P purchases by prosumer j from a different prosumer i at each time t. | Individual P2P sales by prosumer j to a different prosumer i at each time t. | P2P exchanges reported in Fig. 12 at the sample time t=39. | Prosumer purchases from peers and the upstream grid in each time-slot. | Prosumer sales to peers and the upstream grid in each time-slot, using nonpositive signed selling quantities. | Residual upstream-grid exchanges included in the LEM. | The proposed LEM's P2P trading process, including Step 9. | Upstream-grid trading included in the proposed LEM strategy.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E12","v141-b-g02-E27","v141-b-g02-E28","v141-b-g02-E29","v141-b-g02-E30","v141-b-g02-E31","v141-b-g02-E33","v141-b-g02-E34","v141-b-g03-e013","v141-b-g03-e014"]},"constraint_refs":["43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C004"],"decision_anchor":"execution.energy_trade","source_rule_sha256":"6f886284b421760a203dcd266e69537ae7b3451952fb1d55cbec933b14f8be6f","paper":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market","rule_ref":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R006","node_id":"pricing.local","function":"pricing.local","unit_id":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-a-M03-lem::pricing.local","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M03-lem","anchor":false},{"id":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R009::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R009","market_meaning":"The local clearing optimization determines bilateral trading quantities and yields their P2P clearing prices; the energy-equilibrium constraint corresponds to the price multiplier. Scope: Individual bilateral P2P trades in the proposed local energy market. Condition: At the optimal solution of objective (39), using KKT conditions and the relaxation of constraints (36)-(38).","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"pricing","context_text":"Individual bilateral P2P trades in the proposed local energy market.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E12","v141-b-g02-E36"]},"constraint_refs":["43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C001","43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C002","43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C003"],"decision_anchor":"pricing.local","source_rule_sha256":"a4240c8c6790ab393fca4f365ee545ee9a116fa509f8fa6cc7ef3f22720320c0","paper":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market","rule_ref":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R009","node_id":"pricing.local","function":"pricing.local","unit_id":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-a-M03-lem::pricing.local","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M03-lem","anchor":true},{"id":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R001::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","market_meaning":"The aggregator performs local P2P market clearing. Scope: Proposed local P2P energy market embedded with residential shared energy storage. In these scarcity examples, buyers offering higher P2P transaction prices acquire the limited energy. Scope: Reported Case 3 market-equilibrium examples at t = 8 and t = 18. Condition: Total buyer energy demand exceeds total energy supply, and buyers compete for limited energy. A seller’s total P2P transaction amount cannot exceed its initial surplus energy in that period. Scope: Each energy seller and each period in the proposed P2P equilibrium model. Bilateral P2P transaction amounts must be nonnegative. Scope: Every seller–buyer P2P transaction amount in every period. P2P purchases plus grid purchases plus SES discharge must equal the buyer’s energy demand plus SES charging; grid purchases must be nonnegative. Scope: Each energy buyer and each period in the proposed P2P equilibrium model.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Each energy buyer and each period in the proposed P2P equilibrium model. | Each energy seller and each period in the proposed P2P equilibrium model. | Every seller–buyer P2P transaction amount in every period. | Proposed local P2P energy market embedded with residential shared energy storage. | Reported Case 3 market-equilibrium examples at t = 8 and t = 18.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E09","v141-b-g01-E13","v141-b-g01-E14","v141-b-g01-E16","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g03-e12","v141-b-g03-e13"]},"constraint_refs":["49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C002"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.local","source_rule_sha256":"5b0a6ea74646d677bac9ef5735b8532211f481cb19f837d9ad545e0e8fd0cc2c","paper":"49-residential_shared_storage_market","rule_ref":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R001","node_id":"pricing.local","function":"pricing.local","unit_id":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-a-M01::pricing.local","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":false},{"id":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R005::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","market_meaning":"The aggregator is responsible for matching P2P energy transactions. Scope: The proposed local P2P energy trading market embedded with residential shared energy storage. Buyers’ selection of sellers is conditional on relative P2P transaction prices: buyers choose lower-priced sellers and turn to other sellers if a seller raises its price. Scope: The illustrated t = 15 surplus-supply period, including the hypothetical seller-price increase used to explain buyers’ counterparty choices. Condition: Total PV generation exceeds buyers’ total energy demand; buyers compare sellers’ P2P transaction prices and switch away from a seller who raises its price when lower-priced sellers are available.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"matching","context_text":"The illustrated t = 15 surplus-supply period, including the hypothetical seller-price increase used to explain buyers’ counterparty choices. | The proposed local P2P energy trading market embedded with residential shared energy storage.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e003","v141-b-g03-e14","v141-b-g03-e15"]},"constraint_refs":["49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C002"],"decision_anchor":"matching.counterparties","source_rule_sha256":"8d41efa27f22732f3a57ad73d8950fe70d6bca0d8a92e664bd7e712108029b1c","paper":"49-residential_shared_storage_market","rule_ref":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R005","node_id":"pricing.local","function":"pricing.local","unit_id":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-a-M01::pricing.local","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":false},{"id":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R006::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","market_meaning":"Continued consumer participation is conditional on the local P2P transaction price not exceeding TLOU. Scope: Energy-buyer participation in the modeled local P2P market with residential shared energy storage. Condition: The P2P transaction price must not exceed TLOU; buyers quit otherwise. Energy buyers may trade freely with energy sellers and reach a bilaterally satisfactory transaction result. Scope: Energy buyers participating in the modeled local P2P market.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Energy buyers participating in the modeled local P2P market. | Energy-buyer participation in the modeled local P2P market with residential shared energy storage.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E03","v141-b-g03-e04"]},"constraint_refs":["49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001"],"decision_anchor":"participation.consumer","source_rule_sha256":"994b36819e26ccabbe86c561facc4cb1f0a665134ef08996156d526cc28f0433","paper":"49-residential_shared_storage_market","rule_ref":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R006","node_id":"pricing.local","function":"pricing.local","unit_id":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-a-M01::pricing.local","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":false},{"id":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R007::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","market_meaning":"A renewable-energy seller's decision to participate in the P2P transaction market is conditional on the price signal. Scope: Renewable-energy sellers choosing between participation in the proposed local P2P market and trading with the power utility. Energy sellers' continued P2P participation is conditional on the local transaction price satisfying the model's seller-specific lower bound. Scope: Energy sellers' continued participation in the proposed local P2P model. Condition: The P2P transaction price ρ^{t}_{sb} must be at least λ^{t}_{sell} + λ^{t}_{cost}, representing FiT plus the P2P transaction cost; otherwise sellers quit. Energy sellers are permitted to trade freely with energy buyers and reach bilaterally satisfactory transaction results. Scope: Energy-seller participation in the proposed local P2P market. Energy sellers may still sell their surplus to the power grid after participating in the P2P transaction. Scope: Energy sellers participating in the proposed P2P market and selling remaining surplus to the power grid. Condition: After participating in the P2P transaction, when surplus energy remains. Energy sellers are forbidden to buy from the grid and sell to the P2P market simultaneously. Scope: Energy-seller participation in the paper's price-setting setup.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Energy sellers participating in the proposed P2P market and selling remaining surplus to the power grid. | Energy sellers' continued participation in the proposed local P2P model. | Energy-seller participation in the paper's price-setting setup. | Energy-seller participation in the proposed local P2P market. | Renewable-energy sellers choosing between participation in the proposed local P2P market and trading with the power utility.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E03","v141-b-g01-E14","v141-b-g02-e005","v141-b-g02-e040","v141-b-g03-e04"]},"constraint_refs":["49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C002"],"decision_anchor":"participation.prosumer","source_rule_sha256":"cb8c69cda4c7c6dc856b6b19e385cda7fc09dbc40996e2fe7adf359a5e751308","paper":"49-residential_shared_storage_market","rule_ref":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R007","node_id":"pricing.local","function":"pricing.local","unit_id":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-a-M01::pricing.local","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":false},{"id":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R008::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R008","market_meaning":"Solving the local market equilibrium determines P2P transaction allocations and produces the endogenous bilateral transaction prices. Scope: Bilateral seller–buyer transactions in the proposed local P2P market embedded with residential shared energy storage. The P2P transaction price must be no less than the feed-in tariff plus the P2P transaction cost and no greater than TLOU. Scope: Bilateral P2P transaction prices in the proposed model. No single participant unilaterally controls the P2P clearing price. Scope: The proposed local P2P pricing mechanism.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"pricing","context_text":"Bilateral P2P transaction prices in the proposed model. | Bilateral seller–buyer transactions in the proposed local P2P market embedded with residential shared energy storage. | The proposed local P2P pricing mechanism.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E09","v141-b-g01-E13","v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g03-e03","v141-b-g03-e04"]},"constraint_refs":["49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C001","49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C002","49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C003"],"decision_anchor":"pricing.local","source_rule_sha256":"cb8bb8165d1e8d357b66c34b44b5c95ee431f6bab35f04fb44df80f24044ce4d","paper":"49-residential_shared_storage_market","rule_ref":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R008","node_id":"pricing.local","function":"pricing.local","unit_id":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-a-M01::pricing.local","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true}],"paper_ids":["43-operating_envelope_ev_market","49-residential_shared_storage_market"],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E12","location":{"location_id":"src-1fe7e180ad-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M03-matching","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"Overview of the Proposed Framework","anchor_text":"unt Fig. 1. The proposed framework of DOE-based prosumers management in the LEM. ed ith consumption ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Finally, a P2P trading process is executed by a local market operator (LMO) through a matching mechanism to obtain the P2P clearing prices and the quantities of the trading energy with the peers (Step 9).","paper":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"Finally, a P2P trading process is executed by a local market operator (LMO) through a matching mechanism to obtain the P2P clearing prices and the quantities of the trading energy with the peers (Step 9)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e013","location":{"location_id":"src-1fe7e180ad-p11-c1r3__v141-a-M03-matching","page":11,"printed_page":"1722","section":"P2P Trading Analysis","anchor_text":"(¢) corresponding to those tradings are shown in Fig. 12b. As is noticed in Fig. 12, each P2P tradin","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 12","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-11.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"As is noticed in Fig. 12, each P2P trading is executed in a decentralized way, whereby a prosumer acting as a buyer purchases from another prosumer (who plays the role of a seller) at an optimal P2P trading price. Further, a buyer trades with multiple sellers simultaneously and vice versa is also true for a seller in order to meet the required energy quantity ($P_{j,t}^{pb}/P_{j,t}^{ps}$), where tradings are confined by the availability of their buying/selling amounts and constraints. Fig. 12 demonstrates that prosumers are able to manage their P2P tradings optimally by achieving their P2P trading limits and respecting both network and local market constraints.","paper":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"As is noticed in Fig. 12, each P2P trading is executed in a decentralized way, whereby a prosumer acting as a buyer purchases from another prosumer (who plays the role of a seller) at an optimal P2P trading price. Further, a buyer trades with multiple sellers simultaneously and vice versa is also true for a seller in order to meet the required energy quantity ($P_{j,t}^{pb}/P_{j,t}^{ps}$), where tradings are confined by the availability of their buying/selling amounts and constraints. Fig. 12 demonstrates that prosumers are able to manage their P2P tradings optimally by achieving their P2P trading limits and respecting both network and local market constraints."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e014","location":{"location_id":"src-1fe7e180ad-p10-c2r4__v141-a-M03-clearing","page":10,"printed_page":"1721","section":"P2P Trading Analysis","anchor_text":"g- for trading with the P2P and the upstream grid of a sample ed prosumer (j = 27). Results in Fig. ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 12","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-10.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"As explained earlier, prosumers generate their trading bids ($P_{j,i,t}^{pb}/P_{i,j,t}^{ps}$) and prices ($\\lambda_{j,i,t}^{c}$) optimally for the P2P trading coordinated by LMO in the proposed framework. Fig. 12 illustrates the P2P trading results of prosumers (buyers and sellers) at an arbitrarily-chosen sample time $t=39$.","paper":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"As explained earlier, prosumers generate their trading bids ($P_{j,i,t}^{pb}/P_{i,j,t}^{ps}$) and prices ($\\lambda_{j,i,t}^{c}$) optimally for the P2P trading coordinated by LMO in the proposed framework. Fig. 12 illustrates the P2P trading results of prosumers (buyers and sellers) at an arbitrarily-chosen sample time $t=39$."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E12","location":{"location_id":"src-1fe7e180ad-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M03-matching","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"Overview of the Proposed Framework","anchor_text":"unt Fig. 1. The proposed framework of DOE-based prosumers management in the LEM. ed ith consumption ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Finally, a P2P trading process is executed by a local market operator (LMO) through a matching mechanism to obtain the P2P clearing prices and the quantities of the trading energy with the peers (Step 9).","paper":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market","rule_id":"v141-c-R009","quote":"Finally, a P2P trading process is executed by a local market operator (LMO) through a matching mechanism to obtain the P2P clearing prices and the quantities of the trading energy with the peers (Step 9)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E36","location":{"location_id":"src-1fe7e180ad-p08-c1r4__v141-a-M03-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"P2P Trading Mechanism","anchor_text":"energy by i to j. In (39), the third term presents the product of Lagrange multiplier λcj,i,t and th","equation_id":"(39)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The objective function (39) yields P2P trading bids (P^{pb}_{j,i,t} and P^{ps}_{i,j,t}) and a P2P trading price λ^{c}_{j,i,t} of the optimal solution using Karush–Kuhn–Tucker (KKT) conditions, and constraints (36)-(38) are relaxed by Lagrange multiplier λ^{c}_{j,i,t} for attaining lower-extremes of (39). The first and second terms in (39) define the total cost of P2P buying energy by j from i and the total revenue of P2P selling energy by i to j. In (39), the third term presents the product of Lagrange multiplier λ^{c}_{j,i,t} and the equilibrium quantity of P2P buying and selling energy, where the energy equilibrium constraint (38) corresponds to the Lagrange multiplier λ^{c}_{j,i,t} in (39).","paper":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market","rule_id":"v141-c-R009","quote":"The objective function (39) yields P2P trading bids (P^{pb}_{j,i,t} and P^{ps}_{i,j,t}) and a P2P trading price λ^{c}_{j,i,t} of the optimal solution using Karush–Kuhn–Tucker (KKT) conditions, and constraints (36)-(38) are relaxed by Lagrange multiplier λ^{c}_{j,i,t} for attaining lower-extremes of (39). The first and second terms in (39) define the total cost of P2P buying energy by j from i and the total revenue of P2P selling energy by i to j. In (39), the third term presents the product of Lagrange multiplier λ^{c}_{j,i,t} and the equilibrium quantity of P2P buying and selling energy, where the energy equilibrium constraint (38) corresponds to the Lagrange multiplier λ^{c}_{j,i,t} in (39)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E35","location":{"location_id":"src-1fe7e180ad-p08-c1r3__v141-a-M03-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"P2P Trading Mechanism","anchor_text":"min Ppbj,i,t · pbj,t + Ppsi,j,t · psi,t Fig j∈J i∈J |i̸=j −λcj,i,t Ppbj,i,t + Ppsi,j,t τ; Ppsi,j,t ≤","equation_id":"(39)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"To determine the individual P2P trading bids (P^{pb}_{j,i,t} and P^{ps}_{i,j,t}) and the P2P trading prices λ^{c}_{j,i,t} of prosumers (j and i), the optimal decision making problem is devised by the LMO that minimizes the mismatches between individual P2P trades as, min ∑_{j∈J} ∑_{i∈J | i≠j} ((P^{pb}_{j,i,t}·Λ^{pb}_{j,t} + P^{ps}_{i,j,t}·Λ^{ps}_{i,t}) − λ^{c}_{j,i,t}(P^{pb}_{j,i,t} + P^{ps}_{i,j,t})) τ; P^{ps}_{i,j,t} ≤ 0, ∀(j,i) ∈ J | j ≠ i, ∀t∈T, subject to: (36)−(38), (39) where Λ^{pb}_{j,t} and Λ^{ps}_{i,t} are the prices for P2P trading between prosumer j and prosumer i at time t.","paper":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market","rule_id":"v141-c-R009","quote":"To determine the individual P2P trading bids (P^{pb}_{j,i,t} and P^{ps}_{i,j,t}) and the P2P trading prices λ^{c}_{j,i,t} of prosumers (j and i), the optimal decision making problem is devised by the LMO that minimizes the mismatches between individual P2P trades as, min ∑_{j∈J} ∑_{i∈J | i≠j} ((P^{pb}_{j,i,t}·Λ^{pb}_{j,t} + P^{ps}_{i,j,t}·Λ^{ps}_{i,t}) − λ^{c}_{j,i,t}(P^{pb}_{j,i,t} + P^{ps}_{i,j,t})) τ; P^{ps}_{i,j,t} ≤ 0, ∀(j,i) ∈ J | j ≠ i, ∀t∈T, subject to: (36)−(38), (39) where Λ^{pb}_{j,t} and Λ^{ps}_{i,t} are the prices for P2P trading between prosumer j and prosumer i at time t."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e003","location":{"location_id":"src-1fe7e180ad-p08-c1r3__v141-a-M03-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":"1719","section":"P2P Trading Mechanism","anchor_text":"min Ppbj,i,t · pbj,t + Ppsi,j,t · psi,t Fig j∈J i∈J |i̸=j −λcj,i,t Ppbj,i,t + Ppsi,j,t τ; Ppsi,j,t ≤","equation_id":"(39)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"To determine the individual P2P trading bids ($P_{j,i,t}^{pb}$ and $P_{i,j,t}^{ps}$) and the P2P trading prices $\\lambda_{j,i,t}^{c}$ of prosumers ($j$ and $i$), the optimal decision making problem is devised by the LMO that minimizes the mismatches between individual P2P trades as, $\\min \\sum_{j\\in J}\\sum_{i\\in J|i\\ne j} ((P_{j,i,t}^{pb}\\cdot \\Lambda_{j,t}^{pb}+P_{i,j,t}^{ps}\\cdot \\Lambda_{i,t}^{ps})-\\lambda_{j,i,t}^{c}(P_{j,i,t}^{pb}+P_{i,j,t}^{ps})\\tau);$ $P_{i,j,t}^{ps}\\le 0, \\forall (j,i)\\in J|j\\ne i, \\forall t\\in T,$ subject to: (36)-(38), (39).","paper":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market","rule_id":"v141-c-R009","quote":"To determine the individual P2P trading bids ($P_{j,i,t}^{pb}$ and $P_{i,j,t}^{ps}$) and the P2P trading prices $\\lambda_{j,i,t}^{c}$ of prosumers ($j$ and $i$), the optimal decision making problem is devised by the LMO that minimizes the mismatches between individual P2P trades as, $\\min \\sum_{j\\in J}\\sum_{i\\in J|i\\ne j} ((P_{j,i,t}^{pb}\\cdot \\Lambda_{j,t}^{pb}+P_{i,j,t}^{ps}\\cdot \\Lambda_{i,t}^{ps})-\\lambda_{j,i,t}^{c}(P_{j,i,t}^{pb}+P_{i,j,t}^{ps})\\tau);$ $P_{i,j,t}^{ps}\\le 0, \\forall (j,i)\\in J|j\\ne i, \\forall t\\in T,$ subject to: (36)-(38), (39)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e004","location":{"location_id":"src-1fe7e180ad-p08-c1r4__v141-a-M03-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":"1719","section":"P2P Trading Mechanism","anchor_text":"energy by i to j. In (39), the third term presents the product of Lagrange multiplier λcj,i,t and th","equation_id":"(39)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"where $\\Lambda_{j,t}^{pb}$ and $\\Lambda_{i,t}^{ps}$ are the prices for P2P trading between prosumer $j$ and prosumer $i$ at time $t$. The objective function (39) yields P2P trading bids ($P_{j,i,t}^{pb}$ and $P_{i,j,t}^{ps}$) and a P2P trading price $\\lambda_{j,i,t}^{c}$ of the optimal solution using Karush–Kuhn–Tucker (KKT) conditions, and constraints (36)-(38) are relaxed by Lagrange multiplier $\\lambda_{j,i,t}^{c}$ for attaining lower-extremes of (39). The first and second terms in (39) define the total cost of P2P buying energy by $j$ from $i$ and the total revenue of P2P selling energy by $i$ to $j$. In (39), the third term presents the product of Lagrange multiplier $\\lambda_{j,i,t}^{c}$ and the equilibrium quantity of P2P buying and selling energy, where the energy equilibrium constraint (38) corresponds to the Lagrange multiplier $\\lambda_{j,i,t}^{c}$ in (39).","paper":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market","rule_id":"v141-c-R009","quote":"where $\\Lambda_{j,t}^{pb}$ and $\\Lambda_{i,t}^{ps}$ are the prices for P2P trading between prosumer $j$ and prosumer $i$ at time $t$. The objective function (39) yields P2P trading bids ($P_{j,i,t}^{pb}$ and $P_{i,j,t}^{ps}$) and a P2P trading price $\\lambda_{j,i,t}^{c}$ of the optimal solution using Karush–Kuhn–Tucker (KKT) conditions, and constraints (36)-(38) are relaxed by Lagrange multiplier $\\lambda_{j,i,t}^{c}$ for attaining lower-extremes of (39). The first and second terms in (39) define the total cost of P2P buying energy by $j$ from $i$ and the total revenue of P2P selling energy by $i$ to $j$. In (39), the third term presents the product of Lagrange multiplier $\\lambda_{j,i,t}^{c}$ and the equilibrium quantity of P2P buying and selling energy, where the energy equilibrium constraint (38) corresponds to the Lagrange multiplier $\\lambda_{j,i,t}^{c}$ in (39)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E34","location":{"location_id":"src-1fe7e180ad-p08-c1r2__v141-a-M03-matching","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"P2P Trading Mechanism","anchor_text":"0 ≤Ppbj,i,t ≤Ppbj,t Cj,i,t; ∀(j, i) ∈J |j̸ = i, ∀t ∈T , (36) Ppsj,t 1 −Cj,i,t ≤Ppsj,i,t ≤0; ∀(j, i) ","equation_id":"(38)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Equation (38) refers to the energy matching constraint of prosumers' buying and selling in each P2P trade at time t, which implies that the amount of buying energy by prosumer j from prosumer i should be equal to the amount of selling energy by prosumer i to prosumer j at time t. P^{pb}_{j,i,t} + P^{ps}_{i,j,t} = 0; P^{ps}_{i,j,t} ≤ 0, ∀(j,i) ∈ J | i ≠ j, ∀t∈T. (38)","paper":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market","rule_id":"v141-c-R009","quote":"Equation (38) refers to the energy matching constraint of prosumers' buying and selling in each P2P trade at time t, which implies that the amount of buying energy by prosumer j from prosumer i should be equal to the amount of selling energy by prosumer i to prosumer j at time t. P^{pb}_{j,i,t} + P^{ps}_{i,j,t} = 0; P^{ps}_{i,j,t} ≤ 0, ∀(j,i) ∈ J | i ≠ j, ∀t∈T. (38)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e002","location":{"location_id":"src-1fe7e180ad-p08-c1r2__v141-a-M03-matching","page":8,"printed_page":"1719","section":"P2P Trading Mechanism","anchor_text":"0 ≤Ppbj,i,t ≤Ppbj,t Cj,i,t; ∀(j, i) ∈J |j̸ = i, ∀t ∈T , (36) Ppsj,t 1 −Cj,i,t ≤Ppsj,i,t ≤0; ∀(j, i) ","equation_id":"(38)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Equation (38) refers to the energy matching constraint of prosumers’ buying and selling in each P2P trade at time $t$, which implies that the amount of buying energy by prosumer $j$ from prosumer $i$ should be equal to the amount of selling energy by prosumer $i$ to prosumer $j$ at time $t$. $P_{j,i,t}^{pb} + P_{i,j,t}^{ps} = 0; P_{i,j,t}^{ps} \\le 0, \\forall (j,i) \\in J | i \\ne j, \\forall t \\in T. \\quad (38)$","paper":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market","rule_id":"v141-c-R009","quote":"Equation (38) refers to the energy matching constraint of prosumers’ buying and selling in each P2P trade at time $t$, which implies that the amount of buying energy by prosumer $j$ from prosumer $i$ should be equal to the amount of selling energy by prosumer $i$ to prosumer $j$ at time $t$. $P_{j,i,t}^{pb} + P_{i,j,t}^{ps} = 0; P_{i,j,t}^{ps} \\le 0, \\forall (j,i) \\in J | i \\ne j, \\forall t \\in T. \\quad (38)$"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e12","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p10-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":10,"printed_page":null,"section":"5.2.2 Peer-to-peer market equilibrium","anchor_text":"m. as shown in Fig. 3c. However, during the generation peak time, total al supply exceeds total dema","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-10.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"To further show the results of P2P transactions, we pick up 4 typical periods to analyze the P2P market equilibrium in Case 3, as shown in Table 4. The transaction pair is (ρ^{t}_{sb}, p^{t}_{st}), indicating the P2P transaction price and amount between seller s and buyer b during period t. The supply is the PV generation, while the demand is the initial fixed demand plus/minus charging/discharging power of shared energy storage. TLOU0 is the base price faced by buyers when purchasing electricity from the grid. FiT is the Feed-in Tariff offered by the grid. In our model, the P2P transaction price and amount are obtained from solving the market equilibrium problem.","paper":"49-residential_shared_storage_market","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","quote":"To further show the results of P2P transactions, we pick up 4 typical periods to analyze the P2P market equilibrium in Case 3, as shown in Table 4. The transaction pair is (ρ^{t}_{sb}, p^{t}_{st}), indicating the P2P transaction price and amount between seller s and buyer b during period t. The supply is the PV generation, while the demand is the initial fixed demand plus/minus charging/discharging power of shared energy storage. TLOU0 is the base price faced by buyers when purchasing electricity from the grid. FiT is the Feed-in Tariff offered by the grid. In our model, the P2P transaction price and amount are obtained from solving the market equilibrium problem."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e13","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p10-c1r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":10,"printed_page":null,"section":"5.2.2 Peer-to-peer market equilibrium","anchor_text":"market equilibrium problem. Table 4 provides a direct insight into how ac market equilibrium arises.","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-10.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"When t = 8, the PV generation is small, so the total energy demand among energy buyers exceeds the total energy supply. In such a circumstance, all buyers will compete for the limited energy resource. Buyers offering higher transaction prices will acquire the limited energy, e.g., B3 with the highest price 0.1814. Actually, the price 0.1814 is also the marginal cost when B3 buys electricity from the grid to satisfy the unmet needs after the P2P transaction. The same analysis works at t = 18 time period.","paper":"49-residential_shared_storage_market","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","quote":"When t = 8, the PV generation is small, so the total energy demand among energy buyers exceeds the total energy supply. In such a circumstance, all buyers will compete for the limited energy resource. Buyers offering higher transaction prices will acquire the limited energy, e.g., B3 with the highest price 0.1814. Actually, the price 0.1814 is also the marginal cost when B3 buys electricity from the grid to satisfy the unmet needs after the P2P transaction. The same analysis works at t = 18 time period."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e14","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p10-c1r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":10,"printed_page":null,"section":"5.2.2 Peer-to-peer market equilibrium","anchor_text":"market equilibrium problem. Table 4 provides a direct insight into how ac market equilibrium arises.","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-10.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"When t = 15, the total PV generations exceed the total energy demand of buyers, which means the renewable energy generations can not only satisfy all P2P transaction demand but also have to sell excess energy to gird. Given the context, buyers will trade with sellers who require a lower price. And sellers have the motivation to decrease their price in exchange for competitiveness in P2P transactions.","paper":"49-residential_shared_storage_market","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","quote":"When t = 15, the total PV generations exceed the total energy demand of buyers, which means the renewable energy generations can not only satisfy all P2P transaction demand but also have to sell excess energy to gird. Given the context, buyers will trade with sellers who require a lower price. And sellers have the motivation to decrease their price in exchange for competitiveness in P2P transactions."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e15","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p10-c2r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":10,"printed_page":null,"section":"5.2.2 Peer-to-peer market equilibrium","anchor_text":"Applied Energy 308 (2022) 118400 price in exchange for competitiveness in P2P transactions. A conspi","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-10.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"A conspicuous feature is that each buyer purchases energy from different sellers with the same price 0.1350. The reason is that if a seller improves the P2P transaction price, buyers will not buy energy from him and turn to other sellers with lower prices, causing a revenue drop to the aforementioned seller. Besides, the same price is the FiT plus the transaction cost λ^{cost}. FiT can be seen as the marginal revenue of selling energy to the grid, which is identical to all sellers. The same parameter setting for all sellers accounts for the same P2P transaction price.","paper":"49-residential_shared_storage_market","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","quote":"A conspicuous feature is that each buyer purchases energy from different sellers with the same price 0.1350. The reason is that if a seller improves the P2P transaction price, buyers will not buy energy from him and turn to other sellers with lower prices, causing a revenue drop to the aforementioned seller. Besides, the same price is the FiT plus the transaction cost λ^{cost}. FiT can be seen as the marginal revenue of selling energy to the grid, which is identical to all sellers. The same parameter setting for all sellers accounts for the same P2P transaction price."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e04","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p08-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1 KKT conditions / price bounds","anchor_text":"sb ters and some dual variables. This P2P pricing mechanism is beyond the as control of a single par","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"In addition, in our model, ρ^{t}_{sb} must be no less than the λ^{t}_{sell} + λ^{t}_{cost}, otherwise, sellers will quit the P2P market. Similarly, ρ^{t}_{sb} must be no more than TLOU, otherwise, buyers will quit the P2P market.","paper":"49-residential_shared_storage_market","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"In addition, in our model, ρ^{t}_{sb} must be no less than the λ^{t}_{sell} + λ^{t}_{cost}, otherwise, sellers will quit the P2P market. Similarly, ρ^{t}_{sb} must be no more than TLOU, otherwise, buyers will quit the P2P market."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e04","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p08-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1 KKT conditions / price bounds","anchor_text":"sb ters and some dual variables. This P2P pricing mechanism is beyond the as control of a single par","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"In addition, in our model, ρ^{t}_{sb} must be no less than the λ^{t}_{sell} + λ^{t}_{cost}, otherwise, sellers will quit the P2P market. Similarly, ρ^{t}_{sb} must be no more than TLOU, otherwise, buyers will quit the P2P market.","paper":"49-residential_shared_storage_market","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","quote":"In addition, in our model, ρ^{t}_{sb} must be no less than the λ^{t}_{sell} + λ^{t}_{cost}, otherwise, sellers will quit the P2P market. Similarly, ρ^{t}_{sb} must be no more than TLOU, otherwise, buyers will quit the P2P market."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E09","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.3 Aggregator","anchor_text":"Since the energy buyers use the SES, the allocation of SES use right is also a game. In practice, th","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The aggregator collects all participants’ supply and demand information and clears the P2P market. By solving the market equilibrium problem, it can provide P2P transaction price and amount between energy seller and buyer and capacity allocation among energy buyers. The aggregator can facilitate the P2P market and make sure participants benefit from the market.","paper":"49-residential_shared_storage_market","rule_id":"v141-c-R008","quote":"The aggregator collects all participants’ supply and demand information and clears the P2P market. By solving the market equilibrium problem, it can provide P2P transaction price and amount between energy seller and buyer and capacity allocation among energy buyers. The aggregator can facilitate the P2P market and make sure participants benefit from the market."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E23","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p04-c2r1__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"1.3 Contributions","anchor_text":"Applied Energy 308 (2022) 118400 al 2) A solution method based on Karush-Kuhn-Tucker (KKT) optimalit","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"2) A solution method based on Karush-Kuhn-Tucker (KKT) optimality condition is applied to solve the market equilibrium problem. The market equilibrium is acquired based on the KKT conditions of all participants’ optimal problems. After concatenating all KKT conditions together, the market equilibrium is cast as a mixed-integer linear program (MILP) with the complementarity conditions linearized through the Big-M method. The P2P transaction price and the amount and the SES capacity allocation are obtained from the market equilibrium. The P2P transaction price is determined by the supply and demand relationship and reflects the value of energy resources. Capacity allocation among users can ensure fairness in our definition.","paper":"49-residential_shared_storage_market","rule_id":"v141-c-R008","quote":"2) A solution method based on Karush-Kuhn-Tucker (KKT) optimality condition is applied to solve the market equilibrium problem. The market equilibrium is acquired based on the KKT conditions of all participants’ optimal problems. After concatenating all KKT conditions together, the market equilibrium is cast as a mixed-integer linear program (MILP) with the complementarity conditions linearized through the Big-M method. The P2P transaction price and the amount and the SES capacity allocation are obtained from the market equilibrium. The P2P transaction price is determined by the supply and demand relationship and reflects the value of energy resources. Capacity allocation among users can ensure fairness in our definition."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e034","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p08-c1r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1 KKT conditions / P2P price","anchor_text":"B. Zheng et al. constraints. The P2P transaction price variable ρtsb is endogenously determined and ","equation_id":"(46)-(47)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The P2P transaction price variable \\rho_{sb}^{t} is endogenously determined and the SES capacity is allocated by solving equilibrium (45). From the energy sellers’ side, according to equation (28), \\rho_{sb}^{t} can be calculated as follows: \\rho_{sb}^{t} = \\lambda_{sell}^{t} + \\lambda_{cost}^{t} + (\\upsilon_s^{t} - \\omega_{sb}^{t})/\\Delta t \\tag{46} From the energy buyers’ side, according to equation (32), \\rho_{sb}^{t} can be calculated as follows: \\rho_{sb}^{t} = -\\gamma_b^{t}/\\Delta t \\tag{47} The price in (46) and (47) are the same in the equilibrium problem (45), and thus the transaction price \\rho_{sb}^{t} mainly depends on the parameters and some dual variables.","paper":"49-residential_shared_storage_market","rule_id":"v141-c-R008","quote":"The P2P transaction price variable \\rho_{sb}^{t} is endogenously determined and the SES capacity is allocated by solving equilibrium (45). From the energy sellers’ side, according to equation (28), \\rho_{sb}^{t} can be calculated as follows: \\rho_{sb}^{t} = \\lambda_{sell}^{t} + \\lambda_{cost}^{t} + (\\upsilon_s^{t} - \\omega_{sb}^{t})/\\Delta t \\tag{46} From the energy buyers’ side, according to equation (32), \\rho_{sb}^{t} can be calculated as follows: \\rho_{sb}^{t} = -\\gamma_b^{t}/\\Delta t \\tag{47} The price in (46) and (47) are the same in the equilibrium problem (45), and thus the transaction price \\rho_{sb}^{t} mainly depends on the parameters and some dual variables."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E13","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.1 Seller’s problem","anchor_text":"gy , pi to decision-making problem can be cast as ∑ ∑ ∑ ∑ e. max : ( ρtsbptsb + λtsell(Pts − ptsb) −","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"where p^{t}_{sb} and \\rho^{t}_{sb} are decision variables. p^{t}_{sb} is the transaction amount between seller s and buyer b in period t. \\rho^{t}_{sb} is transaction price associated with p^{t}_{sb}. However, the market-clearing price \\rho^{t}_{sb} is out of control of seller s and buyer b but is determined by the market equilibrium problem of the game. Parameter P^{t}_{s} is the renewable energy for sale of seller s in period t. Parameter \\lambda^{t}_{sell} is FiT when a seller sells its energy to the power grid. Parameter \\lambda^{t}_{cost} is the P2P transaction cost, including network tariff for using the physical grid facilities and aggregator service fee.","paper":"49-residential_shared_storage_market","rule_id":"v141-c-R008","quote":"where p^{t}_{sb} and \\rho^{t}_{sb} are decision variables. p^{t}_{sb} is the transaction amount between seller s and buyer b in period t. \\rho^{t}_{sb} is transaction price associated with p^{t}_{sb}. However, the market-clearing price \\rho^{t}_{sb} is out of control of seller s and buyer b but is determined by the market equilibrium problem of the game. Parameter P^{t}_{s} is the renewable energy for sale of seller s in period t. Parameter \\lambda^{t}_{sell} is FiT when a seller sells its energy to the power grid. Parameter \\lambda^{t}_{cost} is the P2P transaction cost, including network tariff for using the physical grid facilities and aggregator service fee."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e04","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p08-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1 KKT conditions / price bounds","anchor_text":"sb ters and some dual variables. This P2P pricing mechanism is beyond the as control of a single par","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"In addition, in our model, ρ^{t}_{sb} must be no less than the λ^{t}_{sell} + λ^{t}_{cost}, otherwise, sellers will quit the P2P market. Similarly, ρ^{t}_{sb} must be no more than TLOU, otherwise, buyers will quit the P2P market.","paper":"49-residential_shared_storage_market","rule_id":"v141-c-R008","quote":"In addition, in our model, ρ^{t}_{sb} must be no less than the λ^{t}_{sell} + λ^{t}_{cost}, otherwise, sellers will quit the P2P market. Similarly, ρ^{t}_{sb} must be no more than TLOU, otherwise, buyers will quit the P2P market."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e03","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p08-c1r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1 KKT conditions / P2P price","anchor_text":"B. Zheng et al. constraints. The P2P transaction price variable ρtsb is endogenously determined and ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The price in (46) and (47) are the same in the equilibrium problem (45), and thus the transaction price ρ^{t}_{sb} mainly depends on the parameters and some dual variables. This P2P pricing mechanism is beyond the control of a single participant, and truly reflects the demand and supply relationship and the true value of energy, which is also fair to both sides.","paper":"49-residential_shared_storage_market","rule_id":"v141-c-R008","quote":"The price in (46) and (47) are the same in the equilibrium problem (45), and thus the transaction price ρ^{t}_{sb} mainly depends on the parameters and some dual variables. This P2P pricing mechanism is beyond the control of a single participant, and truly reflects the demand and supply relationship and the true value of energy, which is also fair to both sides."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e01","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p08-c1r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1 KKT conditions / P2P price","anchor_text":"B. Zheng et al. constraints. The P2P transaction price variable ρtsb is endogenously determined and ","equation_id":"46","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"From the energy sellers’ side, according to equation (28), ρ^{t}_{sb} can be calculated as follows: ρ^{t}_{sb} = λ^{t}_{sell} + λ^{t}_{cost} + (υ^{t}_{s} − ω^{t}_{sb})/Δt    (46)","paper":"49-residential_shared_storage_market","rule_id":"v141-c-R008","quote":"From the energy sellers’ side, according to equation (28), ρ^{t}_{sb} can be calculated as follows: ρ^{t}_{sb} = λ^{t}_{sell} + λ^{t}_{cost} + (υ^{t}_{s} − ω^{t}_{sb})/Δt    (46)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e02","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p08-c1r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1 KKT conditions / P2P price","anchor_text":"B. Zheng et al. constraints. The P2P transaction price variable ρtsb is endogenously determined and ","equation_id":"47","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"From the energy buyers’ side, according to equation (32), ρ^{t}_{sb} can be calculated as follows: ρ^{t}_{sb} = − γ^{t}_{b}/Δt    (47)","paper":"49-residential_shared_storage_market","rule_id":"v141-c-R008","quote":"From the energy buyers’ side, according to equation (32), ρ^{t}_{sb} can be calculated as follows: ρ^{t}_{sb} = − γ^{t}_{b}/Δt    (47)"}],"candidate_only":true,"classification_subtypes":["bilateral_balance_multiplier_pricing","endogenous_bilateral_equilibrium_price"],"projection_statuses":["clear"],"taxonomy":{"primary_family":"kind-L6-42efdbc705","secondary_families":[],"stage":"L6","ontological_kind":"bilateral_balance_multiplier_pricing"},"semantic_definition":{"definition":"In the optimality or equilibrium conditions of the local P2P trading market, endogenously determine the seller-buyer transaction unit price according to the price-dual variable value relationship explicitly stated in the source text.","object":"P2P electricity transaction prices identified by buyer, seller, and time period; retain the price-sharing relationship of each source.","inputs":"The trading-market model, optimality conditions, and dual variables specified by the source, as well as bids, costs, duration, and applicable constraints.","operation":"Extract the price from the trading-market solution according to the dual relationship made explicit by the source, retaining the sign, time normalization, and additional price decomposition of that relationship.","outputs":"Market-endogenous bilateral transaction prices and the price bounds specified by the source.","boundary":"Limited to L6/BCT pricing; does not merge complete clearing, allocation, or payment processes. Different objectives, dual-constraint levels, and price-sharing scopes are not treated as equivalent; 49 does not provide the full text of equation (32), and the parent class does not require binding γ to a particular verified balancing constraint. Excludes ex post discounted pricing using a shadow price from a no-trading baseline."},"source_units":[{"unit_id":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-a-M03-lem::pricing.local","paper_id":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M03-lem","function":"pricing.local","source_occurrences":[{"id":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R006::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","market_meaning":"The submitted total P2P buying-quantity bid limits each individual P2P purchase through the upper bound P^{pb}_{j,t} C_{j,i,t}. Scope: Individual P2P purchases by prosumer j from a different prosumer i at each time t. Condition: Subject to the trading-status factor C_{j,i,t} in (36). The submitted total P2P selling-quantity bid limits each individual P2P sale through the signed lower bound P^{ps}_{j,t}(1 − C_{j,i,t}). Scope: Individual P2P sales by prosumer j to a different prosumer i at each time t. Condition: Subject to the complementary trading-status factor 1 − C_{j,i,t} in (37); selling quantities are nonpositive. The local market operator executes and coordinates P2P trading among prosumers. Scope: The proposed LEM's P2P trading process, including Step 9. The optimal P2P trading price applies to the executed exchange between a buyer and a seller. Scope: P2P exchanges reported in Fig. 12 at the sample time t=39. Combined peer and upstream-grid purchases must not exceed the assigned import DOE. Each peer and grid buying quantity is nonnegative and individually bounded by that DOE. Scope: Prosumer purchases from peers and the upstream grid in each time-slot. Condition: The assigned DOE P̂_{j,t} is positive. Combined signed peer and upstream-grid sales must be at or above the assigned export DOE. Each peer and grid selling quantity lies between that DOE and zero. Scope: Prosumer sales to peers and the upstream grid in each time-slot, using nonpositive signed selling quantities. Condition: The assigned DOE P̂_{j,t} is nonpositive. Total P2P buying and total P2P selling must balance in each time-slot. Scope: Aggregate P2P trading in each time-slot τ. The amount bought by j from i must equal the amount sold by i to j in the same trade and time-slot. Scope: Each individual P2P trade between distinct prosumers j and i at time t. Remaining imports or exports may be traded with the upstream grid. Scope: Residual upstream-grid exchanges included in the LEM. Condition: Imports or exports remain after P2P trading and are within the assigned DOE P̂_{j,t}. The strategy assumes that the grid trade equals the assigned DOE. Scope: Upstream-grid trading included in the proposed LEM strategy. Condition: No P2P trade occurs.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"activation","context_text":"Aggregate P2P trading in each time-slot τ. | Each individual P2P trade between distinct prosumers j and i at time t. | Individual P2P purchases by prosumer j from a different prosumer i at each time t. | Individual P2P sales by prosumer j to a different prosumer i at each time t. | P2P exchanges reported in Fig. 12 at the sample time t=39. | Prosumer purchases from peers and the upstream grid in each time-slot. | Prosumer sales to peers and the upstream grid in each time-slot, using nonpositive signed selling quantities. | Residual upstream-grid exchanges included in the LEM. | The proposed LEM's P2P trading process, including Step 9. | Upstream-grid trading included in the proposed LEM strategy.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E12","v141-b-g02-E27","v141-b-g02-E28","v141-b-g02-E29","v141-b-g02-E30","v141-b-g02-E31","v141-b-g02-E33","v141-b-g02-E34","v141-b-g03-e013","v141-b-g03-e014"]},"constraint_refs":["43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C004"],"decision_anchor":"execution.energy_trade","source_rule_sha256":"6f886284b421760a203dcd266e69537ae7b3451952fb1d55cbec933b14f8be6f"},{"id":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R009::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R009","market_meaning":"The local clearing optimization determines bilateral trading quantities and yields their P2P clearing prices; the energy-equilibrium constraint corresponds to the price multiplier. Scope: Individual bilateral P2P trades in the proposed local energy market. Condition: At the optimal solution of objective (39), using KKT conditions and the relaxation of constraints (36)-(38).","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"pricing","context_text":"Individual bilateral P2P trades in the proposed local energy market.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E12","v141-b-g02-E36"]},"constraint_refs":["43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C001","43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C002","43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C003"],"decision_anchor":"pricing.local","source_rule_sha256":"a4240c8c6790ab393fca4f365ee545ee9a116fa509f8fa6cc7ef3f22720320c0"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C004","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The optimal P2P trading price applies to the executed exchange between a buyer and a seller. Scope: P2P exchanges reported in Fig. 12 at the sample time t=39.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local","execution.energy_trade"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E12","v141-b-g03-e013","v141-b-g03-e014"],"ref":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C004","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E12","location":{"location_id":"src-1fe7e180ad-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M03-matching","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"Overview of the Proposed Framework","anchor_text":"unt Fig. 1. The proposed framework of DOE-based prosumers management in the LEM. ed ith consumption ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Finally, a P2P trading process is executed by a local market operator (LMO) through a matching mechanism to obtain the P2P clearing prices and the quantities of the trading energy with the peers (Step 9)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e013","location":{"location_id":"src-1fe7e180ad-p11-c1r3__v141-a-M03-matching","page":11,"printed_page":"1722","section":"P2P Trading Analysis","anchor_text":"(¢) corresponding to those tradings are shown in Fig. 12b. As is noticed in Fig. 12, each P2P tradin","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 12","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-11.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"As is noticed in Fig. 12, each P2P trading is executed in a decentralized way, whereby a prosumer acting as a buyer purchases from another prosumer (who plays the role of a seller) at an optimal P2P trading price. Further, a buyer trades with multiple sellers simultaneously and vice versa is also true for a seller in order to meet the required energy quantity ($P_{j,t}^{pb}/P_{j,t}^{ps}$), where tradings are confined by the availability of their buying/selling amounts and constraints. Fig. 12 demonstrates that prosumers are able to manage their P2P tradings optimally by achieving their P2P trading limits and respecting both network and local market constraints."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e014","location":{"location_id":"src-1fe7e180ad-p10-c2r4__v141-a-M03-clearing","page":10,"printed_page":"1721","section":"P2P Trading Analysis","anchor_text":"g- for trading with the P2P and the upstream grid of a sample ed prosumer (j = 27). Results in Fig. ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 12","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-10.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"As explained earlier, prosumers generate their trading bids ($P_{j,i,t}^{pb}/P_{i,j,t}^{ps}$) and prices ($\\lambda_{j,i,t}^{c}$) optimally for the P2P trading coordinated by LMO in the proposed framework. Fig. 12 illustrates the P2P trading results of prosumers (buyers and sellers) at an arbitrarily-chosen sample time $t=39$."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R009-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The local clearing optimization determines bilateral trading quantities and yields their P2P clearing prices; the energy-equilibrium constraint corresponds to the price multiplier. Scope: Individual bilateral P2P trades in the proposed local energy market. Condition: At the optimal solution of objective (39), using KKT conditions and the relaxation of constraints (36)-(38).","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E12","v141-b-g02-E36"],"ref":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E12","location":{"location_id":"src-1fe7e180ad-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M03-matching","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"Overview of the Proposed Framework","anchor_text":"unt Fig. 1. The proposed framework of DOE-based prosumers management in the LEM. ed ith consumption ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Finally, a P2P trading process is executed by a local market operator (LMO) through a matching mechanism to obtain the P2P clearing prices and the quantities of the trading energy with the peers (Step 9)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E36","location":{"location_id":"src-1fe7e180ad-p08-c1r4__v141-a-M03-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"P2P Trading Mechanism","anchor_text":"energy by i to j. In (39), the third term presents the product of Lagrange multiplier λcj,i,t and th","equation_id":"(39)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The objective function (39) yields P2P trading bids (P^{pb}_{j,i,t} and P^{ps}_{i,j,t}) and a P2P trading price λ^{c}_{j,i,t} of the optimal solution using Karush–Kuhn–Tucker (KKT) conditions, and constraints (36)-(38) are relaxed by Lagrange multiplier λ^{c}_{j,i,t} for attaining lower-extremes of (39). The first and second terms in (39) define the total cost of P2P buying energy by j from i and the total revenue of P2P selling energy by i to j. In (39), the third term presents the product of Lagrange multiplier λ^{c}_{j,i,t} and the equilibrium quantity of P2P buying and selling energy, where the energy equilibrium constraint (38) corresponds to the Lagrange multiplier λ^{c}_{j,i,t} in (39)."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R009-C002","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"Formal transcription of the core local-clearing optimization in Eq. (39): the LMO minimizes, over individual bilateral P2P buying and selling quantities and the P2P trading price multiplier, the peer buying-cost and selling-revenue terms net of the multiplier-weighted equilibrium quantity, subject to (36)-(38).","expression":"\\min \\sum_{j\\in J}\\sum_{i\\in J\\,|\\,i\\ne j} \\left( \\left(P^{pb}_{j,i,t}\\Lambda^{pb}_{j,t}+P^{ps}_{i,j,t}\\Lambda^{ps}_{i,t}\\right)-\\lambda^{c}_{j,i,t}\\left(P^{pb}_{j,i,t}+P^{ps}_{i,j,t}\\right) \\right)\\tau;\\quad P^{ps}_{i,j,t}\\le 0,\\ \\forall (j,i)\\in J\\,|\\,j\\ne i,\\ \\forall t\\in T,\\quad \\text{subject to (36)-(38)}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R009-x-Ppb-jit","v141-c-R009-x-Pps-ijt","v141-c-R009-x-lambda-cijt"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R009-theta-Lambda-pb-jt","v141-c-R009-theta-Lambda-ps-it","v141-c-R009-theta-tau"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E35","v141-b-g02-E36","v141-b-g03-e003","v141-b-g03-e004"],"ref":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E35","location":{"location_id":"src-1fe7e180ad-p08-c1r3__v141-a-M03-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"P2P Trading Mechanism","anchor_text":"min Ppbj,i,t · pbj,t + Ppsi,j,t · psi,t Fig j∈J i∈J |i̸=j −λcj,i,t Ppbj,i,t + Ppsi,j,t τ; Ppsi,j,t ≤","equation_id":"(39)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"To determine the individual P2P trading bids (P^{pb}_{j,i,t} and P^{ps}_{i,j,t}) and the P2P trading prices λ^{c}_{j,i,t} of prosumers (j and i), the optimal decision making problem is devised by the LMO that minimizes the mismatches between individual P2P trades as, min ∑_{j∈J} ∑_{i∈J | i≠j} ((P^{pb}_{j,i,t}·Λ^{pb}_{j,t} + P^{ps}_{i,j,t}·Λ^{ps}_{i,t}) − λ^{c}_{j,i,t}(P^{pb}_{j,i,t} + P^{ps}_{i,j,t})) τ; P^{ps}_{i,j,t} ≤ 0, ∀(j,i) ∈ J | j ≠ i, ∀t∈T, subject to: (36)−(38), (39) where Λ^{pb}_{j,t} and Λ^{ps}_{i,t} are the prices for P2P trading between prosumer j and prosumer i at time t."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E36","location":{"location_id":"src-1fe7e180ad-p08-c1r4__v141-a-M03-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"P2P Trading Mechanism","anchor_text":"energy by i to j. In (39), the third term presents the product of Lagrange multiplier λcj,i,t and th","equation_id":"(39)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The objective function (39) yields P2P trading bids (P^{pb}_{j,i,t} and P^{ps}_{i,j,t}) and a P2P trading price λ^{c}_{j,i,t} of the optimal solution using Karush–Kuhn–Tucker (KKT) conditions, and constraints (36)-(38) are relaxed by Lagrange multiplier λ^{c}_{j,i,t} for attaining lower-extremes of (39). The first and second terms in (39) define the total cost of P2P buying energy by j from i and the total revenue of P2P selling energy by i to j. In (39), the third term presents the product of Lagrange multiplier λ^{c}_{j,i,t} and the equilibrium quantity of P2P buying and selling energy, where the energy equilibrium constraint (38) corresponds to the Lagrange multiplier λ^{c}_{j,i,t} in (39)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e003","location":{"location_id":"src-1fe7e180ad-p08-c1r3__v141-a-M03-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":"1719","section":"P2P Trading Mechanism","anchor_text":"min Ppbj,i,t · pbj,t + Ppsi,j,t · psi,t Fig j∈J i∈J |i̸=j −λcj,i,t Ppbj,i,t + Ppsi,j,t τ; Ppsi,j,t ≤","equation_id":"(39)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"To determine the individual P2P trading bids ($P_{j,i,t}^{pb}$ and $P_{i,j,t}^{ps}$) and the P2P trading prices $\\lambda_{j,i,t}^{c}$ of prosumers ($j$ and $i$), the optimal decision making problem is devised by the LMO that minimizes the mismatches between individual P2P trades as, $\\min \\sum_{j\\in J}\\sum_{i\\in J|i\\ne j} ((P_{j,i,t}^{pb}\\cdot \\Lambda_{j,t}^{pb}+P_{i,j,t}^{ps}\\cdot \\Lambda_{i,t}^{ps})-\\lambda_{j,i,t}^{c}(P_{j,i,t}^{pb}+P_{i,j,t}^{ps})\\tau);$ $P_{i,j,t}^{ps}\\le 0, \\forall (j,i)\\in J|j\\ne i, \\forall t\\in T,$ subject to: (36)-(38), (39)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e004","location":{"location_id":"src-1fe7e180ad-p08-c1r4__v141-a-M03-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":"1719","section":"P2P Trading Mechanism","anchor_text":"energy by i to j. In (39), the third term presents the product of Lagrange multiplier λcj,i,t and th","equation_id":"(39)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"where $\\Lambda_{j,t}^{pb}$ and $\\Lambda_{i,t}^{ps}$ are the prices for P2P trading between prosumer $j$ and prosumer $i$ at time $t$. The objective function (39) yields P2P trading bids ($P_{j,i,t}^{pb}$ and $P_{i,j,t}^{ps}$) and a P2P trading price $\\lambda_{j,i,t}^{c}$ of the optimal solution using Karush–Kuhn–Tucker (KKT) conditions, and constraints (36)-(38) are relaxed by Lagrange multiplier $\\lambda_{j,i,t}^{c}$ for attaining lower-extremes of (39). The first and second terms in (39) define the total cost of P2P buying energy by $j$ from $i$ and the total revenue of P2P selling energy by $i$ to $j$. In (39), the third term presents the product of Lagrange multiplier $\\lambda_{j,i,t}^{c}$ and the equilibrium quantity of P2P buying and selling energy, where the energy equilibrium constraint (38) corresponds to the Lagrange multiplier $\\lambda_{j,i,t}^{c}$ in (39)."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R009-C003","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"Energy-equilibrium constraint (38): the P2P energy bought by prosumer j from prosumer i equals the negative of the P2P energy sold by prosumer i to prosumer j, with nonpositive selling quantities. The source explicitly states that this constraint corresponds to the Lagrange multiplier λ^c_{j,i,t} in (39).","expression":"P^{pb}_{j,i,t}+P^{ps}_{i,j,t}=0;\\quad P^{ps}_{i,j,t}\\le 0,\\quad \\forall (j,i)\\in J\\,|\\,i\\ne j,\\ \\forall t\\in T","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R009-x-Ppb-jit","v141-c-R009-x-Pps-ijt","v141-c-R009-x-lambda-cijt"],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E34","v141-b-g02-E36","v141-b-g03-e002","v141-b-g03-e004"],"ref":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E34","location":{"location_id":"src-1fe7e180ad-p08-c1r2__v141-a-M03-matching","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"P2P Trading Mechanism","anchor_text":"0 ≤Ppbj,i,t ≤Ppbj,t Cj,i,t; ∀(j, i) ∈J |j̸ = i, ∀t ∈T , (36) Ppsj,t 1 −Cj,i,t ≤Ppsj,i,t ≤0; ∀(j, i) ","equation_id":"(38)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Equation (38) refers to the energy matching constraint of prosumers' buying and selling in each P2P trade at time t, which implies that the amount of buying energy by prosumer j from prosumer i should be equal to the amount of selling energy by prosumer i to prosumer j at time t. P^{pb}_{j,i,t} + P^{ps}_{i,j,t} = 0; P^{ps}_{i,j,t} ≤ 0, ∀(j,i) ∈ J | i ≠ j, ∀t∈T. (38)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E36","location":{"location_id":"src-1fe7e180ad-p08-c1r4__v141-a-M03-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"P2P Trading Mechanism","anchor_text":"energy by i to j. In (39), the third term presents the product of Lagrange multiplier λcj,i,t and th","equation_id":"(39)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The objective function (39) yields P2P trading bids (P^{pb}_{j,i,t} and P^{ps}_{i,j,t}) and a P2P trading price λ^{c}_{j,i,t} of the optimal solution using Karush–Kuhn–Tucker (KKT) conditions, and constraints (36)-(38) are relaxed by Lagrange multiplier λ^{c}_{j,i,t} for attaining lower-extremes of (39). The first and second terms in (39) define the total cost of P2P buying energy by j from i and the total revenue of P2P selling energy by i to j. In (39), the third term presents the product of Lagrange multiplier λ^{c}_{j,i,t} and the equilibrium quantity of P2P buying and selling energy, where the energy equilibrium constraint (38) corresponds to the Lagrange multiplier λ^{c}_{j,i,t} in (39)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e002","location":{"location_id":"src-1fe7e180ad-p08-c1r2__v141-a-M03-matching","page":8,"printed_page":"1719","section":"P2P Trading Mechanism","anchor_text":"0 ≤Ppbj,i,t ≤Ppbj,t Cj,i,t; ∀(j, i) ∈J |j̸ = i, ∀t ∈T , (36) Ppsj,t 1 −Cj,i,t ≤Ppsj,i,t ≤0; ∀(j, i) ","equation_id":"(38)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Equation (38) refers to the energy matching constraint of prosumers’ buying and selling in each P2P trade at time $t$, which implies that the amount of buying energy by prosumer $j$ from prosumer $i$ should be equal to the amount of selling energy by prosumer $i$ to prosumer $j$ at time $t$. $P_{j,i,t}^{pb} + P_{i,j,t}^{ps} = 0; P_{i,j,t}^{ps} \\le 0, \\forall (j,i) \\in J | i \\ne j, \\forall t \\in T. \\quad (38)$"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e004","location":{"location_id":"src-1fe7e180ad-p08-c1r4__v141-a-M03-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":"1719","section":"P2P Trading Mechanism","anchor_text":"energy by i to j. In (39), the third term presents the product of Lagrange multiplier λcj,i,t and th","equation_id":"(39)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"where $\\Lambda_{j,t}^{pb}$ and $\\Lambda_{i,t}^{ps}$ are the prices for P2P trading between prosumer $j$ and prosumer $i$ at time $t$. The objective function (39) yields P2P trading bids ($P_{j,i,t}^{pb}$ and $P_{i,j,t}^{ps}$) and a P2P trading price $\\lambda_{j,i,t}^{c}$ of the optimal solution using Karush–Kuhn–Tucker (KKT) conditions, and constraints (36)-(38) are relaxed by Lagrange multiplier $\\lambda_{j,i,t}^{c}$ for attaining lower-extremes of (39). The first and second terms in (39) define the total cost of P2P buying energy by $j$ from $i$ and the total revenue of P2P selling energy by $i$ to $j$. In (39), the third term presents the product of Lagrange multiplier $\\lambda_{j,i,t}^{c}$ and the equilibrium quantity of P2P buying and selling energy, where the energy equilibrium constraint (38) corresponds to the Lagrange multiplier $\\lambda_{j,i,t}^{c}$ in (39)."}]}],"variables":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R009-x-Ppb-jit","symbol":"P^{pb}_{j,i,t}","meaning":"Individual bilateral P2P buying quantity/energy bid by prosumer j from prosumer i at time t, used in the LMO optimization (39) and bounded by the prosumer's total P2P buying bid and binary trading status in (36).","kind":"operational","domain":"Nonnegative real in the clearing constraints; 0 ≤ P^{pb}_{j,i,t} ≤ P^{pb}_{j,t} C_{j,i,t} for j≠i and t∈T (Eq. 36). Fig. 12 reports traded amounts in kW.","unit":"kW","indices":["j∈J","i∈J, i≠j","t∈T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E33","v141-b-g02-E35","v141-b-g02-E36","v141-b-g03-e003"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","declaration_ref":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R009::x::v141-c-R009-x-Ppb-jit"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R009-x-Pps-ijt","symbol":"P^{ps}_{i,j,t}","meaning":"Individual bilateral P2P selling quantity by prosumer i to prosumer j at time t, with the subscript order used in Eqs. (38)-(39). The source uses P^{ps}_{j,i,t} in (37) and P^{ps}_{i,j,t} in (38)-(39); the exact cross-subscript identification is noted for revision.","kind":"operational","domain":"Nonpositive real; P^{ps}_{i,j,t} ≤ 0 for (j,i)∈J with i≠j and t∈T (Eqs. 38-39). The matching bound in (37) is stated with P^{ps}_{j,i,t} in the supplied text.","unit":"kW","indices":["i∈J","j∈J, i≠j","t∈T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E33","v141-b-g02-E34","v141-b-g02-E35","v141-b-g02-E36","v141-b-g03-e002"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","declaration_ref":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R009::x::v141-c-R009-x-Pps-ijt"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R009-x-lambda-cijt","symbol":"\\lambda^{c}_{j,i,t}","meaning":"P2P trading price between prosumers j and i at time t, obtained as the price of the optimal solution of LMO optimization (39) using KKT conditions and identified in the source as the Lagrange multiplier corresponding to energy-equilibrium constraint (38).","kind":"operational","domain":"Real-valued price; the supplied text does not impose an explicit sign restriction on λ^c_{j,i,t}.","unit":"price unit as printed in Fig. 12 (¢); consistent with the \\Lambda price terms in (39)","indices":["j∈J","i∈J, i≠j","t∈T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E35","v141-b-g02-E36","v141-b-g03-e003","v141-b-g03-e004"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","declaration_ref":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R009::x::v141-c-R009-x-lambda-cijt"}],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R009-theta-Lambda-pb-jt","symbol":"\\Lambda^{pb}_{j,t}","meaning":"Price for P2P trading in the buying term of objective (39), associated with prosumer j at time t; the source defines \\Lambda^{pb}_{j,t} and \\Lambda^{ps}_{i,t} as the prices for P2P trading between prosumer j and prosumer i at time t.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"price unit (¢ as printed in Fig. 12)","indices":["j∈J","t∈T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E35","v141-b-g02-E36","v141-b-g03-e004"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","declaration_ref":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R009::theta::v141-c-R009-theta-Lambda-pb-jt"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R009-theta-Lambda-ps-it","symbol":"\\Lambda^{ps}_{i,t}","meaning":"Price for P2P trading in the selling term of objective (39), associated with prosumer i at time t; the source defines \\Lambda^{pb}_{j,t} and \\Lambda^{ps}_{i,t} as the prices for P2P trading between prosumer j and prosumer i at time t.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"price unit (¢ as printed in Fig. 12)","indices":["i∈J","t∈T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E35","v141-b-g02-E36","v141-b-g03-e004"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","declaration_ref":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R009::theta::v141-c-R009-theta-Lambda-ps-it"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R009-theta-tau","symbol":"\\tau","meaning":"Time-slot duration/factor multiplying the double-sum objective in (39); the supplied source uses τ for trading intervals but does not state a general numerical value in this rule's evidence.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"time","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E35"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","declaration_ref":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R009::theta::v141-c-R009-theta-tau"}]},{"unit_id":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-a-M01::pricing.local","paper_id":"49-residential_shared_storage_market","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"pricing.local","source_occurrences":[{"id":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R001::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","market_meaning":"The aggregator performs local P2P market clearing. Scope: Proposed local P2P energy market embedded with residential shared energy storage. In these scarcity examples, buyers offering higher P2P transaction prices acquire the limited energy. Scope: Reported Case 3 market-equilibrium examples at t = 8 and t = 18. Condition: Total buyer energy demand exceeds total energy supply, and buyers compete for limited energy. A seller’s total P2P transaction amount cannot exceed its initial surplus energy in that period. Scope: Each energy seller and each period in the proposed P2P equilibrium model. Bilateral P2P transaction amounts must be nonnegative. Scope: Every seller–buyer P2P transaction amount in every period. P2P purchases plus grid purchases plus SES discharge must equal the buyer’s energy demand plus SES charging; grid purchases must be nonnegative. Scope: Each energy buyer and each period in the proposed P2P equilibrium model.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Each energy buyer and each period in the proposed P2P equilibrium model. | Each energy seller and each period in the proposed P2P equilibrium model. | Every seller–buyer P2P transaction amount in every period. | Proposed local P2P energy market embedded with residential shared energy storage. | Reported Case 3 market-equilibrium examples at t = 8 and t = 18.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E09","v141-b-g01-E13","v141-b-g01-E14","v141-b-g01-E16","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g03-e12","v141-b-g03-e13"]},"constraint_refs":["49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C002"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.local","source_rule_sha256":"5b0a6ea74646d677bac9ef5735b8532211f481cb19f837d9ad545e0e8fd0cc2c"},{"id":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R005::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","market_meaning":"The aggregator is responsible for matching P2P energy transactions. Scope: The proposed local P2P energy trading market embedded with residential shared energy storage. Buyers’ selection of sellers is conditional on relative P2P transaction prices: buyers choose lower-priced sellers and turn to other sellers if a seller raises its price. Scope: The illustrated t = 15 surplus-supply period, including the hypothetical seller-price increase used to explain buyers’ counterparty choices. Condition: Total PV generation exceeds buyers’ total energy demand; buyers compare sellers’ P2P transaction prices and switch away from a seller who raises its price when lower-priced sellers are available.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"matching","context_text":"The illustrated t = 15 surplus-supply period, including the hypothetical seller-price increase used to explain buyers’ counterparty choices. | The proposed local P2P energy trading market embedded with residential shared energy storage.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e003","v141-b-g03-e14","v141-b-g03-e15"]},"constraint_refs":["49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C002"],"decision_anchor":"matching.counterparties","source_rule_sha256":"8d41efa27f22732f3a57ad73d8950fe70d6bca0d8a92e664bd7e712108029b1c"},{"id":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R006::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","market_meaning":"Continued consumer participation is conditional on the local P2P transaction price not exceeding TLOU. Scope: Energy-buyer participation in the modeled local P2P market with residential shared energy storage. Condition: The P2P transaction price must not exceed TLOU; buyers quit otherwise. Energy buyers may trade freely with energy sellers and reach a bilaterally satisfactory transaction result. Scope: Energy buyers participating in the modeled local P2P market.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Energy buyers participating in the modeled local P2P market. | Energy-buyer participation in the modeled local P2P market with residential shared energy storage.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E03","v141-b-g03-e04"]},"constraint_refs":["49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001"],"decision_anchor":"participation.consumer","source_rule_sha256":"994b36819e26ccabbe86c561facc4cb1f0a665134ef08996156d526cc28f0433"},{"id":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R007::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","market_meaning":"A renewable-energy seller's decision to participate in the P2P transaction market is conditional on the price signal. Scope: Renewable-energy sellers choosing between participation in the proposed local P2P market and trading with the power utility. Energy sellers' continued P2P participation is conditional on the local transaction price satisfying the model's seller-specific lower bound. Scope: Energy sellers' continued participation in the proposed local P2P model. Condition: The P2P transaction price ρ^{t}_{sb} must be at least λ^{t}_{sell} + λ^{t}_{cost}, representing FiT plus the P2P transaction cost; otherwise sellers quit. Energy sellers are permitted to trade freely with energy buyers and reach bilaterally satisfactory transaction results. Scope: Energy-seller participation in the proposed local P2P market. Energy sellers may still sell their surplus to the power grid after participating in the P2P transaction. Scope: Energy sellers participating in the proposed P2P market and selling remaining surplus to the power grid. Condition: After participating in the P2P transaction, when surplus energy remains. Energy sellers are forbidden to buy from the grid and sell to the P2P market simultaneously. Scope: Energy-seller participation in the paper's price-setting setup.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Energy sellers participating in the proposed P2P market and selling remaining surplus to the power grid. | Energy sellers' continued participation in the proposed local P2P model. | Energy-seller participation in the paper's price-setting setup. | Energy-seller participation in the proposed local P2P market. | Renewable-energy sellers choosing between participation in the proposed local P2P market and trading with the power utility.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E03","v141-b-g01-E14","v141-b-g02-e005","v141-b-g02-e040","v141-b-g03-e04"]},"constraint_refs":["49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C002"],"decision_anchor":"participation.prosumer","source_rule_sha256":"cb8c69cda4c7c6dc856b6b19e385cda7fc09dbc40996e2fe7adf359a5e751308"},{"id":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R008::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R008","market_meaning":"Solving the local market equilibrium determines P2P transaction allocations and produces the endogenous bilateral transaction prices. Scope: Bilateral seller–buyer transactions in the proposed local P2P market embedded with residential shared energy storage. The P2P transaction price must be no less than the feed-in tariff plus the P2P transaction cost and no greater than TLOU. Scope: Bilateral P2P transaction prices in the proposed model. No single participant unilaterally controls the P2P clearing price. Scope: The proposed local P2P pricing mechanism.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"pricing","context_text":"Bilateral P2P transaction prices in the proposed model. | Bilateral seller–buyer transactions in the proposed local P2P market embedded with residential shared energy storage. | The proposed local P2P pricing mechanism.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E09","v141-b-g01-E13","v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g03-e03","v141-b-g03-e04"]},"constraint_refs":["49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C001","49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C002","49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C003"],"decision_anchor":"pricing.local","source_rule_sha256":"cb8bb8165d1e8d357b66c34b44b5c95ee431f6bab35f04fb44df80f24044ce4d"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R001-C002","kind":"logical","role":"core","statement":"In these scarcity examples, buyers offering higher P2P transaction prices acquire the limited energy. Scope: Reported Case 3 market-equilibrium examples at t = 8 and t = 18. Condition: Total buyer energy demand exceeds total energy supply, and buyers compete for limited energy.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e12","v141-b-g03-e13"],"ref":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e12","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p10-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":10,"printed_page":null,"section":"5.2.2 Peer-to-peer market equilibrium","anchor_text":"m. as shown in Fig. 3c. However, during the generation peak time, total al supply exceeds total dema","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-10.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"To further show the results of P2P transactions, we pick up 4 typical periods to analyze the P2P market equilibrium in Case 3, as shown in Table 4. The transaction pair is (ρ^{t}_{sb}, p^{t}_{st}), indicating the P2P transaction price and amount between seller s and buyer b during period t. The supply is the PV generation, while the demand is the initial fixed demand plus/minus charging/discharging power of shared energy storage. TLOU0 is the base price faced by buyers when purchasing electricity from the grid. FiT is the Feed-in Tariff offered by the grid. In our model, the P2P transaction price and amount are obtained from solving the market equilibrium problem."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e13","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p10-c1r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":10,"printed_page":null,"section":"5.2.2 Peer-to-peer market equilibrium","anchor_text":"market equilibrium problem. Table 4 provides a direct insight into how ac market equilibrium arises.","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-10.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"When t = 8, the PV generation is small, so the total energy demand among energy buyers exceeds the total energy supply. In such a circumstance, all buyers will compete for the limited energy resource. Buyers offering higher transaction prices will acquire the limited energy, e.g., B3 with the highest price 0.1814. Actually, the price 0.1814 is also the marginal cost when B3 buys electricity from the grid to satisfy the unmet needs after the P2P transaction. The same analysis works at t = 18 time period."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C002","kind":"logical","role":"core","statement":"Buyers’ selection of sellers is conditional on relative P2P transaction prices: buyers choose lower-priced sellers and turn to other sellers if a seller raises its price. Scope: The illustrated t = 15 surplus-supply period, including the hypothetical seller-price increase used to explain buyers’ counterparty choices. Condition: Total PV generation exceeds buyers’ total energy demand; buyers compare sellers’ P2P transaction prices and switch away from a seller who raises its price when lower-priced sellers are available.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties","pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e14","v141-b-g03-e15"],"ref":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e14","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p10-c1r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":10,"printed_page":null,"section":"5.2.2 Peer-to-peer market equilibrium","anchor_text":"market equilibrium problem. Table 4 provides a direct insight into how ac market equilibrium arises.","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-10.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"When t = 15, the total PV generations exceed the total energy demand of buyers, which means the renewable energy generations can not only satisfy all P2P transaction demand but also have to sell excess energy to gird. Given the context, buyers will trade with sellers who require a lower price. And sellers have the motivation to decrease their price in exchange for competitiveness in P2P transactions."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e15","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p10-c2r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":10,"printed_page":null,"section":"5.2.2 Peer-to-peer market equilibrium","anchor_text":"Applied Energy 308 (2022) 118400 price in exchange for competitiveness in P2P transactions. A conspi","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-10.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"A conspicuous feature is that each buyer purchases energy from different sellers with the same price 0.1350. The reason is that if a seller improves the P2P transaction price, buyers will not buy energy from him and turn to other sellers with lower prices, causing a revenue drop to the aforementioned seller. Besides, the same price is the FiT plus the transaction cost λ^{cost}. FiT can be seen as the marginal revenue of selling energy to the grid, which is identical to all sellers. The same parameter setting for all sellers accounts for the same P2P transaction price."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C001","kind":"logical","role":"core","statement":"Continued consumer participation is conditional on the local P2P transaction price not exceeding TLOU. Scope: Energy-buyer participation in the modeled local P2P market with residential shared energy storage. Condition: The P2P transaction price must not exceed TLOU; buyers quit otherwise.","expression":"\\rho^{t}_{sb} \\le \\mathrm{TLOU}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["participation.consumer","pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R006-x-rho-sb-t"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R006-theta-TLOU"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e04"],"ref":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e04","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p08-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1 KKT conditions / price bounds","anchor_text":"sb ters and some dual variables. This P2P pricing mechanism is beyond the as control of a single par","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"In addition, in our model, ρ^{t}_{sb} must be no less than the λ^{t}_{sell} + λ^{t}_{cost}, otherwise, sellers will quit the P2P market. Similarly, ρ^{t}_{sb} must be no more than TLOU, otherwise, buyers will quit the P2P market."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R007-C002","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Energy sellers' continued P2P participation is conditional on the local transaction price satisfying the model's seller-specific lower bound. Scope: Energy sellers' continued participation in the proposed local P2P model. Condition: The P2P transaction price ρ^{t}_{sb} must be at least λ^{t}_{sell} + λ^{t}_{cost}, representing FiT plus the P2P transaction cost; otherwise sellers quit.","expression":"\\rho^{t}_{sb} \\ge \\lambda^{t}_{sell} + \\lambda^{t}_{cost}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["participation.prosumer","pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R007-V-rho"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R007-T-lambda_sell","v141-c-R007-T-lambda_cost"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e04"],"ref":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e04","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p08-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1 KKT conditions / price bounds","anchor_text":"sb ters and some dual variables. This P2P pricing mechanism is beyond the as control of a single par","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"In addition, in our model, ρ^{t}_{sb} must be no less than the λ^{t}_{sell} + λ^{t}_{cost}, otherwise, sellers will quit the P2P market. Similarly, ρ^{t}_{sb} must be no more than TLOU, otherwise, buyers will quit the P2P market."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R008-C001","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Solving the local market equilibrium determines P2P transaction allocations and produces the endogenous bilateral transaction prices. Scope: Bilateral seller–buyer transactions in the proposed local P2P market embedded with residential shared energy storage.","expression":"\\text{Equilibrium (45) determines } p^{t}_{sb} \\text{ and } \\rho^{t}_{sb}, \\text{ with } \\rho^{t}_{sb}=\\lambda^{t}_{sell}+\\lambda^{t}_{cost}+(\\upsilon^{t}_{s}-\\omega^{t}_{sb})/\\Delta t=-\\gamma^{t}_{b}/\\Delta t","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R008-x-rho","v141-c-R008-x-p"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R008-theta-Delta-t","v141-c-R008-theta-lambda-sell","v141-c-R008-theta-lambda-cost"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E09","v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g02-e034"],"ref":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R008","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E09","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.3 Aggregator","anchor_text":"Since the energy buyers use the SES, the allocation of SES use right is also a game. In practice, th","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The aggregator collects all participants’ supply and demand information and clears the P2P market. By solving the market equilibrium problem, it can provide P2P transaction price and amount between energy seller and buyer and capacity allocation among energy buyers. The aggregator can facilitate the P2P market and make sure participants benefit from the market."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E23","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p04-c2r1__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"1.3 Contributions","anchor_text":"Applied Energy 308 (2022) 118400 al 2) A solution method based on Karush-Kuhn-Tucker (KKT) optimalit","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"2) A solution method based on Karush-Kuhn-Tucker (KKT) optimality condition is applied to solve the market equilibrium problem. The market equilibrium is acquired based on the KKT conditions of all participants’ optimal problems. After concatenating all KKT conditions together, the market equilibrium is cast as a mixed-integer linear program (MILP) with the complementarity conditions linearized through the Big-M method. The P2P transaction price and the amount and the SES capacity allocation are obtained from the market equilibrium. The P2P transaction price is determined by the supply and demand relationship and reflects the value of energy resources. Capacity allocation among users can ensure fairness in our definition."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e034","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p08-c1r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1 KKT conditions / P2P price","anchor_text":"B. Zheng et al. constraints. The P2P transaction price variable ρtsb is endogenously determined and ","equation_id":"(46)-(47)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The P2P transaction price variable \\rho_{sb}^{t} is endogenously determined and the SES capacity is allocated by solving equilibrium (45). From the energy sellers’ side, according to equation (28), \\rho_{sb}^{t} can be calculated as follows: \\rho_{sb}^{t} = \\lambda_{sell}^{t} + \\lambda_{cost}^{t} + (\\upsilon_s^{t} - \\omega_{sb}^{t})/\\Delta t \\tag{46} From the energy buyers’ side, according to equation (32), \\rho_{sb}^{t} can be calculated as follows: \\rho_{sb}^{t} = -\\gamma_b^{t}/\\Delta t \\tag{47} The price in (46) and (47) are the same in the equilibrium problem (45), and thus the transaction price \\rho_{sb}^{t} mainly depends on the parameters and some dual variables."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R008-C002","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The P2P transaction price must be no less than the feed-in tariff plus the P2P transaction cost and no greater than TLOU. Scope: Bilateral P2P transaction prices in the proposed model.","expression":"\\lambda^{t}_{sell}+\\lambda^{t}_{cost}\\le\\rho^{t}_{sb}\\le \\mathrm{TLOU}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R008-x-rho"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R008-theta-lambda-sell","v141-c-R008-theta-lambda-cost","v141-c-R008-theta-TLOU"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E13","v141-b-g03-e04"],"ref":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R008","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E13","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.1 Seller’s problem","anchor_text":"gy , pi to decision-making problem can be cast as ∑ ∑ ∑ ∑ e. max : ( ρtsbptsb + λtsell(Pts − ptsb) −","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"where p^{t}_{sb} and \\rho^{t}_{sb} are decision variables. p^{t}_{sb} is the transaction amount between seller s and buyer b in period t. \\rho^{t}_{sb} is transaction price associated with p^{t}_{sb}. However, the market-clearing price \\rho^{t}_{sb} is out of control of seller s and buyer b but is determined by the market equilibrium problem of the game. Parameter P^{t}_{s} is the renewable energy for sale of seller s in period t. Parameter \\lambda^{t}_{sell} is FiT when a seller sells its energy to the power grid. Parameter \\lambda^{t}_{cost} is the P2P transaction cost, including network tariff for using the physical grid facilities and aggregator service fee."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e04","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p08-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1 KKT conditions / price bounds","anchor_text":"sb ters and some dual variables. This P2P pricing mechanism is beyond the as control of a single par","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"In addition, in our model, ρ^{t}_{sb} must be no less than the λ^{t}_{sell} + λ^{t}_{cost}, otherwise, sellers will quit the P2P market. Similarly, ρ^{t}_{sb} must be no more than TLOU, otherwise, buyers will quit the P2P market."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R008-C003","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"No single participant unilaterally controls the P2P clearing price. Scope: The proposed local P2P pricing mechanism.","expression":"\\rho^{t}_{sb}=\\lambda^{t}_{sell}+\\lambda^{t}_{cost}+(\\upsilon^{t}_{s}-\\omega^{t}_{sb})/\\Delta t=-\\gamma^{t}_{b}/\\Delta t","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R008-x-rho"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R008-theta-Delta-t","v141-c-R008-theta-lambda-sell","v141-c-R008-theta-lambda-cost"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e03","v141-b-g02-e034","v141-b-g03-e01","v141-b-g03-e02"],"ref":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R008","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e03","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p08-c1r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1 KKT conditions / P2P price","anchor_text":"B. Zheng et al. constraints. The P2P transaction price variable ρtsb is endogenously determined and ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The price in (46) and (47) are the same in the equilibrium problem (45), and thus the transaction price ρ^{t}_{sb} mainly depends on the parameters and some dual variables. This P2P pricing mechanism is beyond the control of a single participant, and truly reflects the demand and supply relationship and the true value of energy, which is also fair to both sides."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e034","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p08-c1r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1 KKT conditions / P2P price","anchor_text":"B. Zheng et al. constraints. The P2P transaction price variable ρtsb is endogenously determined and ","equation_id":"(46)-(47)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The P2P transaction price variable \\rho_{sb}^{t} is endogenously determined and the SES capacity is allocated by solving equilibrium (45). From the energy sellers’ side, according to equation (28), \\rho_{sb}^{t} can be calculated as follows: \\rho_{sb}^{t} = \\lambda_{sell}^{t} + \\lambda_{cost}^{t} + (\\upsilon_s^{t} - \\omega_{sb}^{t})/\\Delta t \\tag{46} From the energy buyers’ side, according to equation (32), \\rho_{sb}^{t} can be calculated as follows: \\rho_{sb}^{t} = -\\gamma_b^{t}/\\Delta t \\tag{47} The price in (46) and (47) are the same in the equilibrium problem (45), and thus the transaction price \\rho_{sb}^{t} mainly depends on the parameters and some dual variables."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e01","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p08-c1r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1 KKT conditions / P2P price","anchor_text":"B. Zheng et al. constraints. The P2P transaction price variable ρtsb is endogenously determined and ","equation_id":"46","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"From the energy sellers’ side, according to equation (28), ρ^{t}_{sb} can be calculated as follows: ρ^{t}_{sb} = λ^{t}_{sell} + λ^{t}_{cost} + (υ^{t}_{s} − ω^{t}_{sb})/Δt    (46)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e02","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p08-c1r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1 KKT conditions / P2P price","anchor_text":"B. Zheng et al. constraints. The P2P transaction price variable ρtsb is endogenously determined and ","equation_id":"47","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"From the energy buyers’ side, according to equation (32), ρ^{t}_{sb} can be calculated as follows: ρ^{t}_{sb} = − γ^{t}_{b}/Δt    (47)"}]}],"variables":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R006-x-rho-sb-t","symbol":"\\rho^{t}_{sb}","meaning":"P2P transaction price between seller s and buyer b in period t; the source calls it a decision variable and also states it is endogenous and not controlled by a single participant. The supplied source does not state a numerical domain beyond its role as a price.","kind":"operational","domain":"P2P transaction price between seller s and buyer b in period t; numerical domain not explicitly specified in the supplied evidence.","unit":null,"indices":["s","b","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g03-e04"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","declaration_ref":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R006::x::v141-c-R006-x-rho-sb-t"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R007-V-rho","symbol":"\\rho^{t}_{sb}","meaning":"Endogenous P2P transaction price between seller s and buyer b in period t; the source states it is not controlled unilaterally by a single seller or buyer and is determined by the market equilibrium.","kind":"operational","domain":"Unspecified in source; indexed by seller s, buyer b, and period t as the P2P transaction price.","unit":null,"indices":["s","b","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E13","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g03-e04"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","declaration_ref":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R007::x::v141-c-R007-V-rho"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R008-x-rho","symbol":"\\rho^{t}_{sb}","meaning":"P2P transaction price between seller s and buyer b in period t; the market-clearing price is endogenous to the market equilibrium and is not unilaterally controlled by seller s or buyer b.","kind":"operational","domain":"Endogenous scalar price. The source supplies participation bounds but no explicit set notation: \\rho^{t}_{sb} \\ge \\lambda^{t}_{sell}+\\lambda^{t}_{cost} and \\rho^{t}_{sb} \\le TLOU.","unit":null,"indices":["s","b","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E13","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g02-e024","v141-b-g02-e034","v141-b-g03-e03"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R008","declaration_ref":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R008::x::v141-c-R008-x-rho"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R008-x-p","symbol":"p^{t}_{sb}","meaning":"P2P transaction amount between seller s and buyer b in period t; an allocation variable determined together with the P2P transaction price by the market equilibrium.","kind":"operational","domain":"Nonnegative transaction amount; the source imposes p^{t}_{sb}\\ge 0 and \\sum_{b\\in B}p^{t}_{sb}\\le P^{t}_{s} in the seller problem.","unit":null,"indices":["s","b","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E11","v141-b-g01-E12","v141-b-g01-E13","v141-b-g01-E21"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R008","declaration_ref":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R008::x::v141-c-R008-x-p"}],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R006-theta-TLOU","symbol":"TLOU","meaning":"Time-and-level-of-use price ceiling in the buyer-participation condition: the source states that the P2P transaction price must be no more than TLOU, otherwise buyers will quit the P2P market. Its numeric value, unit and indexing are not specified in the supplied bound quote.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e04","v141-b-g02-e008","v141-b-g01-E21"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","declaration_ref":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R006::theta::v141-c-R006-theta-TLOU"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R007-T-lambda_sell","symbol":"\\lambda^{t}_{sell}","meaning":"Feed-in tariff in period t; the seller's grid-sale opportunity-cost term in the P2P price floor.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E13","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g03-e04"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","declaration_ref":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R007::theta::v141-c-R007-T-lambda_sell"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R007-T-lambda_cost","symbol":"\\lambda^{t}_{cost}","meaning":"P2P transaction cost in period t, including network tariff for using physical grid facilities and an aggregator service fee.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E13","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g03-e04"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","declaration_ref":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R007::theta::v141-c-R007-T-lambda_cost"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R008-theta-Delta-t","symbol":"\\Delta t","meaning":"Duration of a period, used in the seller-side and buyer-side price decompositions.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g02-e034"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R008","declaration_ref":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R008::theta::v141-c-R008-theta-Delta-t"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R008-theta-lambda-sell","symbol":"\\lambda^{t}_{sell}","meaning":"Feed-in Tariff in period t; lower-bound component for the P2P transaction price.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E13","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g03-e04"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R008","declaration_ref":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R008::theta::v141-c-R008-theta-lambda-sell"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R008-theta-lambda-cost","symbol":"\\lambda^{t}_{cost}","meaning":"P2P transaction cost in period t, including network tariff for using physical grid facilities and aggregator service fee; lower-bound component for the P2P transaction price.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E13","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g03-e04"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R008","declaration_ref":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R008::theta::v141-c-R008-theta-lambda-cost"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R008-theta-TLOU","symbol":"\\mathrm{TLOU}","meaning":"Time-and-level-of-use price referenced by the source as the upper bound on the P2P transaction price; the source writes this bound as TLOU without an explicit indexed symbol in the bound statement.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g03-e04","v141-b-g02-e008"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R008","declaration_ref":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R008::theta::v141-c-R008-theta-TLOU"}]}],"source_definitions":[{"unit_id":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-a-M03-lem::pricing.local","stage":"L6","subtype":"bilateral_balance_multiplier_pricing","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"The transaction-pair-specific P2P price λ^c_{j,i,t} is obtained from the KKT conditions of the optimal solution of equation (39); the source explicitly corresponds it to the Lagrange multiplier of the bilateral energy balance P^{pb}_{j,i,t}+P^{ps}_{i,j,t}=0 and uses this price for exchange between buyer and seller.","form_kind":"mathematical","identity_fields":["Object: bilateral P2P transaction price","Core relationship: price formed from the optimal multiplier corresponding to the bilateral energy balance constraint","Output: λ^c_{j,i,t} indexed by j, i, and t"],"variation_fields":["Uses the KKT conditions stated by the source and the relaxation of constraints (36)-(38)","Λ^{pb}_{j,t} and Λ^{ps}_{i,t} are price parameters in equation (39)","Retain the position of τ in each source's objective","No explicit sign restriction is given for the price","t=39 in Fig.12 is a price application example"],"constraint_refs":["43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C004","43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C001","43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C002","43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C003"],"paper_id":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M03-lem","function":"pricing.local","template_code":"BCT"},{"unit_id":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-a-M01::pricing.local","stage":"L6","subtype":"endogenous_bilateral_equilibrium_price","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"The bilateral transaction price is endogenously determined by market equilibrium; the source formula is \\rho^t_{sb}=\\lambda^t_{sell}+\\lambda^t_{cost}+(\\upsilon^t_s-\\omega^t_{sb})/\\Delta t=-\\gamma^t_b/\\Delta t, and it satisfies \\lambda^t_{sell}+\\lambda^t_{cost}\\le\\rho^t_{sb}\\le\\mathrm{TLOU}; a single participant cannot unilaterally control this price.","form_kind":"mathematical","identity_fields":["Object: seller-buyer bilateral transaction unit price","Core relationship: the relationship between price and dual variables in market equilibrium","Output: endogenous bilateral price ρ^t_sb","Price bounds: lower bound of FiT plus transaction fee and upper bound of TLOU"],"variation_fields":["Seller, buyer, and trading-period indices s, b, t","Parameters λ^t_sell, λ^t_cost, and time-period length Δt","Seller-constraint dual variables υ^t_s and ω^t_sb, and buyer-balance dual variable γ^t_b","Scarcity competition and low-price seller selection are price-use scenarios","The same-price result at t=15 depends on the same parameter settings in the source text"],"constraint_refs":["49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C002","49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C002","49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001","49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C002","49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C001","49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C002","49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C003"],"paper_id":"49-residential_shared_storage_market","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"pricing.local","template_code":"BCT"}],"source_variants":[{"unit_id":"43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-a-M03-lem::pricing.local","source_definition":"The transaction-pair-specific P2P price λ^c_{j,i,t} is obtained from the KKT conditions of the optimal solution of equation (39); the source explicitly corresponds it to the Lagrange multiplier of the bilateral energy balance P^{pb}_{j,i,t}+P^{ps}_{i,j,t}=0 and uses this price for exchange between buyer and seller.","retained_variants":["Transaction-pair index for buyer j, seller i, and time period t; does not add 49's buyer-level price-sharing relationship.","The bilateral balance is P^{pb}_{j,i,t}+P^{ps}_{i,j,t}=0, and the sold quantity is non-positive; retain the subscript discrepancy of the original text's cross-form sold quantity.","Retain the buying cost, selling revenue, and multiplier-weighted balance term of LMO equation (39), and do not equate them with 49's participant equilibrium objective.","Retain the KKT and the relaxation of constraints (36)–(38) as stated by the source; Λ^{pb} and Λ^{ps} are price parameters and are not conflated with the output λ^c.","Retain the position of τ in each citation, and do not use it to unify the time normalization of the two sources; no explicit sign restriction is given for the price.","t=39 in Fig.12 is an example of price application, not a general pricing time restriction."],"constraint_refs":["43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C004","43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C001","43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C002","43-operating_envelope_ev_market::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C003"]},{"unit_id":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-a-M01::pricing.local","source_definition":"The bilateral transaction price is endogenously determined by market equilibrium; the source formula is \\rho^t_{sb}=\\lambda^t_{sell}+\\lambda^t_{cost}+(\\upsilon^t_s-\\omega^t_{sb})/\\Delta t=-\\gamma^t_b/\\Delta t, and it satisfies \\lambda^t_{sell}+\\lambda^t_{cost}\\le\\rho^t_{sb}\\le\\mathrm{TLOU}; a single participant cannot unilaterally control this price.","retained_variants":["The KKT conditions of all participants' optimization problems are combined to form an equilibrium, and the complementarity conditions are linearized via Big-M to convert to a MILP; this is not equivalent to 43's LMO objective.","Fully retain the seller decomposition λ_sell+λ_cost+(υ−ω)/Δt and the buyer expression −γ_b^t/Δt; the signs and duration factors are not rewritten.","ρ carries s, b, and t indices, but the right-hand side of equation (47) contains only b and t; one must not assume, on the basis of the bilateral indices, that every transaction pair has an independent price degree of freedom.","The price lower bound is FiT plus the transaction fee, and the upper bound is TLOU; the transaction fee includes the network facility usage fee and the aggregator service fee.","A single buyer or seller cannot unilaterally control the equilibrium price; the buyer and seller exit bounds are retained separately.","Allocation to the high-price buyer under scarcity and selection of the low-price seller under surplus are examples of price use; the same-price explanation at t=15 depends on identical parameter settings.","The equilibrium simultaneously produces transaction quantities and SES capacity allocation, but this member projects only price formation and does not include energy-storage capacity prices in the object."],"constraint_refs":["49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C002","49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C002","49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001","49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C002","49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C001","49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C002","49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C003"]}],"source_parent_definition":{"definition":"In the optimality or equilibrium conditions of the local P2P trading market, endogenously determine the seller-buyer transaction unit price according to the price-dual variable value relationship explicitly stated in the source text.","object":"P2P electricity transaction prices identified by buyer, seller, and time period; retain the price-sharing relationship of each source.","inputs":"The trading-market model, optimality conditions, and dual variables specified by the source, as well as bids, costs, duration, and applicable constraints.","operation":"Extract the price from the trading-market solution according to the dual relationship made explicit by the source, retaining the sign, time normalization, and additional price decomposition of that relationship.","outputs":"Market-endogenous bilateral transaction prices and the price bounds specified by the source.","boundary":"Limited to L6/BCT pricing; does not merge complete clearing, allocation, or payment processes. Different objectives, dual-constraint levels, and price-sharing scopes are not treated as equivalent; 49 does not provide the full text of equation (32), and the parent class does not require binding γ to a particular verified balancing constraint. Excludes ex post discounted pricing using a shadow price from a no-trading baseline."},"functional":{"is_child":false,"subdivision_pending":false},"canonical_pointer":"/nodes/113"});
